{"id":"route-66","title":"Route 66","who":"Classic route · America's Main Street by motorcycle","when":"May–June and September–October","vehicle":"Motorcycle (a Harley, traditionally), 2–3 weeks","kind":"moto","color":"#b91c1c","summary":"About 3,900 km from Chicago to the Pacific through eight states, on what is left of the 1926 highway: diners, motels and gas stations frozen in time, the plains, the desert Southwest and the Mojave. The road turns 100 in 2026; old alignments run beside the interstates.","sources":[{"label":"Rider Magazine: recollections of a Route 66 motorcycle ride (July 2025)","url":"https://ridermagazine.com/2025/07/17/yore-mother-road-recollections-of-a-route-66-motorcycle-ride/"},{"label":"Reflections Around the Campfire: Route 66 start to finish (October 2025)","url":"https://reflectionsaroundthecampfire.blogspot.com/2025/10/route-66-start-to-finish-chicago-to.html"}],"approximate":"The towns along Route 66; the line follows today's roads, often the interstate beside the old one.","km":3465,"bbox":[-118.4973,34.0101,-87.6144,41.8822],"stops":[{"name":"Chicago","place":"Grant Park, Chicago","at":[41.8827,-87.6187],"text":"The \"Begin Route 66\" sign on Adams Street."},{"name":"Pontiac","place":"Pontiac, Illinois","at":[40.8809,-88.6298],"text":"Murals and the Route 66 Hall of Fame museum.","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Pontiac_City_Hall_and_Fire_Station14.JPG/500px-Pontiac_City_Hall_and_Fire_Station14.JPG","page":"Pontiac, Illinois","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pontiac_City_Hall_and_Fire_Station14.JPG","credit":"IvoShandor","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}},{"name":"Springfield, IL","place":"Springfield, Illinois","at":[39.799,-89.644],"text":"Lincoln's home town and the Cozy Dog drive-in.","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Dana-Thomas_House%2C_Lawrence_Avenue_and_4th_Street%2C_Springfield%2C_IL_-_53322374502.jpg/500px-Dana-Thomas_House%2C_Lawrenc